WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:09.485
OK, so last lecture, I gave
an overview of language models

00:00:09.485 --> 00:00:11.360
and what it means to
build them from scratch,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.750
and why we want to do that.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.430
I also talked
about tokenization,

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.930
which is going to be the first
half of the first assignment.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:22.220
Today's lecture, we'll
be going through actually

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.170
building a model.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.790
We'll discuss the primitives
in PyTorch that are needed.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.780
We're going to
start with tensors,

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.420
build models, optimizers,
and training loop.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:37.560
And we're going to place
close attention to efficiency,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:41.210
in particular, how we're
using resources both memory

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:43.160
and compute.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:48.800
So to motivate things a
bit, here's some questions.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.650
These questions are going to
be answerable by napkin math.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.010
So get your napkins out.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:58.370
So how long would it take to
train a 70 billion parameter

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:04.580
dense transformer model on 15
trillion tokens on 1,024 H100s?

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.325
OK, so I'm just
going to sketch out

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:09.950
the-- give you a flavor
of the type of things

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.160
that we want to do.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.540
So here's how you go
about reasoning it.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:22.230
You count the total number
of flops needed to train.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.560
So that's 6 times the
number of parameters,

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.900
times the number of tokens.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.590
And where does that come from?

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:32.090
That will be what we'll
talk about in this lecture.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.570
You can look at the promise,
the number of flops per second

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.870
that H100 gives you.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.160
The MFU, which is
something we'll see later.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.820
Let's just set it to 0.5.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.560
And you can look at the
number of flops per day

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.690
that your hardware is going to
give you at this particular MFU.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:02:01.312
So 1,024 of them for one
day, and then you just divide

00:02:01.312 --> 00:02:03.520
the total number of flops
you need to train the model

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.400
by the number of flops that
you're supposed to get.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.070
And that gives you about 144.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.590
So this is very
simple calculations.

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.590
At the end of the
day, we're going

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:22.288
to go through a bit more
where these numbers come from.

00:02:22.288 --> 00:02:23.830
And in particular
where the six times

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:29.620
number of parameters times
number of tokens comes from.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:31.420
So here's a question,
what is the largest

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.050
model you can train
on 8 H100s using AdamW

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:37.990
if you're not being too clever?

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:45.670
So H100 has 80
gigabytes of HBM memory.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:47.920
The number of
bytes per parameter

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.200
that you need for the
parameters, the gradients,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.180
optimizer, state is 16.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.960
And we'll talk more about
where that comes from.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.103
And the number of
parameters is basically,

00:02:59.103 --> 00:03:00.520
the total amount
of memory divided

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.230
by number of bytes you
need per parameter.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:09.430
And that gives you about
40 billion parameters.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.700
And this is very
rough because it

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.400
doesn't take you into
activations which

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.920
depends on batch size and
sequence length, which I'm not

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.540
really going to talk
about but will be

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.585
important for assignment one.

00:03:22.585 --> 00:03:25.603
So this is a rough back
envelope calculation.

00:03:25.603 --> 00:03:27.520
And this is something
that you're probably not

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:28.520
used to doing.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.230
You just implement a model,
you train it and what happens

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:31.730
happens.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.280
But remember that efficiency
is the name of the game.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.940
And to be efficient, you have
to know exactly how many flops

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.360
you're actually expending.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.430
Because when these
numbers get large,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.290
these directly
translate into dollars,

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:46.150
and you want that to be
as small as possible.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:48.970
So we'll talk more
about the details

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.143
of how these numbers arise.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:57.130
We will not actually go
over the transformer.

00:03:57.130 --> 00:04:02.220
So Tatsu is going to talk
over the conceptual overview

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.110
of that next time.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.990
And there's many ways you can
learn about transformer if you

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:08.920
haven't already looked at it.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:10.770
There's assignment 1
If you do assignment 1,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.170
you'll definitely know
what a transformer is.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.240
And the handout actually
does a pretty good job

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.200
of walking through all
the different pieces.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.690
There's a mathematical
description

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.470
if you like pictures,
there's pictures.

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:24.520
There's a lot of stuff
you can look on online.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.120
But instead I'm going to
work with simpler models

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.330
and really talk about the
primitives and the resource

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.110
accounting piece.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.380
So remember, last time I
said what kinds of knowledge

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.470
can you learn?

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.020
So mechanics.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.500
In this lecture, it's
going to be just PyTorch

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.480
and understanding
how PyTorch works

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.220
at a fairly primitive level.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.110
So that will be pretty
straightforward.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.110
Mindset is about
resource accounting.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.740
And it's not hard, you
just have to do it.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.190
And intuitions,
unfortunately this

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:58.358
is just going to be
broad strokes for now.

00:04:58.358 --> 00:05:00.150
Actually, there's not
really much intuition

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.950
that I'm going to
talk about in terms

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:05.470
of how anything we're doing
translates to good models.

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:09.810
This is more about the
mechanics and mindset.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:14.790
So let's start with
memory accounting.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:17.400
And then I'll talk about
compute accounting.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.750
And then we'll
build up bottom up.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:20.890
OK.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:24.010
So the best place to
start is a tensor.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.510
So tensors are the building
block for storing everything

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.380
in deep learning.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:32.200
Parameters, gradients, optimizer
state, data, activations.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.050
There's these atoms.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.590
You can read lots of
documentation about them.

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:40.960
You're probably very familiar
with how to create tensors.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:44.160
There's creating
tensors different ways.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.070
You can also create a
tensor and not initialize it

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.540
and use some special
initialization

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.910
for the parameters, if you want.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:58.054
So those are tensors.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:06.000
So let's talk about memory and
how much memory tensors take up.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.490
So every tensor that will
probably be interested in

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:12.540
is as a floating point number.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.720
And so there's many ways to
represent floating point.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.450
So the most default
way is float32.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:24.060
And float32 has 32 bits.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.020
They're allocated one for
sign, eight for exponent,

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:29.970
and 23 for the fraction.

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:32.010
So exponent gives
you dynamic range,

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.660
and fraction gives you
different basically specifies

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:37.430
different values.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:43.550
So float32 is also known
as FP32 or single precision

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.960
is the gold standard
on computing.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:52.530
Some people also refer to
float32 as full precision.

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:55.670
That's a little bit confusing
because full is really depending

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.380
on who you're talking to.

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.730
If you're talking to a
scientific computing person,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.090
they will laugh at
when you say float32

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:07.085
is really full because they'll
use float64 or even more.

00:07:07.085 --> 00:07:09.210
But if you're talking to
a machine learning person,

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.690
float32 is the max you'll
ever probably need to go.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:17.240
Because deep learning
is sloppy like that.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.920
OK, so let's look at the memory.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:21.975
So the memory is
it's very simple.

00:07:21.975 --> 00:07:23.600
It's determined by
the number of values

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.480
you have in your tensor and
the data type of each value

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.060
OK, so if you create
a Torch tensor of 4

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:35.900
by 8 matrix, the default it
will give you a type of float32.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:42.210
The size is 4 by 8 and the
number of elements is 32.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:47.840
Each element size is 4
bytes, 32 bits is 4 bytes.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:51.080
And the memory usage
is simply the number

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:55.170
of elements times the number
of size of each element,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.150
and that will give
you 128 bytes.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.330
OK, so this should
be pretty easy.

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.360
And just to give some
intuition, if you

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:06.350
get the one matrix in the
feed forward layer of GPT-3

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.800
is this number by this
number, and that gives you

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:11.610
2.3 gigabytes.

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:14.060
So that's one matrix is--

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:18.470
these matrices
can be pretty big.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:24.770
So float32 is the default.
But of course, these matrices

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:25.380
get big.

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.010
So you naturally you want
to make them smaller,

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:30.323
so you use less memory.

00:08:30.323 --> 00:08:32.240
And also it turns out
if you make them smaller

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.656
you also make it go faster too.

00:08:36.656 --> 00:08:41.510
So another type
of representation

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:42.750
is called float16.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:47.290
And as the name
suggests, it's 16 bits

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.090
where both the exponent
and the fraction

00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:56.710
are shrunk down from
8 to 5 and 23 to 10.

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.720
So this is known
as half precision

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.230
and it cuts down
half the memory.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:08.080
And that's all great,
except for the dynamic range

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.850
for these float16 isn't great.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:17.620
So for example, if you try to
make a number like 1e minus 8

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:24.340
in float16, it basically
rounds down to zero and you get

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:25.690
underflow.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:29.650
OK, so the float16 is not
great for representing

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:34.030
very small numbers or very big
numbers, as a matter of fact.

00:09:34.030 --> 00:09:39.110
So if you use float16 for
training, for small models

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.650
it's probably going to be OK,
but for large models when you're

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:45.440
having lots of
matrices and you can

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:51.200
get instability or underflow or
overflow and bad things happen.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.730
So one thing that has
happened, which is nice,

00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:00.870
is there's been another
representation of bfloat16,

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:02.930
which stands for brain float.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:06.200
This was developed in
2018 to address the issue

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.980
that for deep
learning, we actually

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:13.130
care about dynamic
range more than we

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:14.820
care about this fraction.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.170
So basically bf16 allocates
more to the exponent

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:20.180
and less to the fraction.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:24.570
So it uses the same memory
as floating point 16,

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:28.340
but it has a dynamic
range of float 32.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.500
So that sounds really good.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:34.170
And it actually-- the catch
is that this resolution,

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:36.240
which is determined by
the fraction is worse.

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.390
But this doesn't matter
as much for deep learning.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:46.000
So now if you try to create a
tensor with 1e minus 8 and bf16,

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.155
then you get something
that's not zero.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:53.827
So you can-- till I
dive into the details,

00:10:53.827 --> 00:10:55.410
I'm not going to go
into this, but you

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.750
can stare at the
actual full specs

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.530
of all the different
floating point operations.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:06.270
So bf16 is basically
what you will typically

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:11.280
use to do computations
because it's good enough

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:12.910
for feedforward computations.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:17.770
It turns out that for storing
optimizer states and parameters,

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:21.840
you still need
float32, otherwise

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.040
your training will go haywire.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.390
So if you're bald.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:33.310
So now we have something
called FP8 or 8-bit.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:40.510
And as the name suggests, this
was developed in 2022 by NVIDIA.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:46.920
So now they have essentially
if you look at FP and bf16

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:47.830
it's like this.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:48.810
And FP--

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:52.600
Wow, you really don't have that
many bits to store stuff right.

00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:54.480
So it's very crude.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.540
There's two variants
depending on if you

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:02.580
want to have more resolution
or more dynamic range.

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:05.740
And I'm not going to
say too much about this,

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:08.080
but FP8 is supported by H100.

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:12.720
It's not really available
on previous generation,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.060
but at a high level, training
with float32, which is, I think

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.490
is what you would do if you're
not trying to optimize too much

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:25.360
and it's safe.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:27.810
It requires more memory.

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:34.350
You can go down to
FP8 or bf16, but you

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.050
can get some instability.

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.940
Basically, I don't
think you would probably

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:42.085
want to use a float16 at
this point for deep learning.

00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:49.910
And you can become more
sophisticated by looking

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:53.820
at particular places
in your pipeline,

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:57.950
either forward pass or backward
pass or optimizers or gradient

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:00.860
accumulation, and
really figure out what

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:04.350
the minimum precision you need
at this particular places.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:07.470
And that's called-- gets into
mixed precision training.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:10.100
So for example, some
people like to use

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:14.000
float32 for the
attention to make sure

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:16.850
that doesn't get messed up.

00:13:16.850 --> 00:13:19.830
But for simple feedforward
passes with matmuls,

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:23.690
bf16 is fine.

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:27.890
OK, pause a bit for questions.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:31.070
So we talked about
tensors and we looked at

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:34.410
depending on what
representation,

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:37.100
how much storage they take.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:37.700
Yeah.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.630
Can you just clarify
about the mixed position.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.200
When you would use
the 32 and the b4?

00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:50.090
Yeah so the question is, when
would you use float32 or bf16?

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:53.460
I don't have time to get
into the exact details,

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:56.010
and it varies depending on
the model size and everything.

00:13:56.010 --> 00:14:00.780
But generally for the
parameters and optimizer states,

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:01.920
you use float32.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.470
You can think about
bf16 as something

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.430
that's more transitory.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:09.680
You basically take your
parameters, you cast it to bf16

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.970
and you run ahead
with that model.

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:14.910
But then the thing that you're
going to accumulate over time,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:17.930
you want to have
higher precision.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:20.870
Yeah.

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:25.380
OK, so now let's
talk about compute.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.980
So that was memory.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:34.770
So compute obviously depends
on what the hardware is.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.930
By default, tensors
are stored in CPU.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:39.650
So for example if
you just in PyTorch,

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:44.130
say x equals Torch 0 is 3232,
then it'll put it on your CPU,

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:47.630
it'll be in the CPU memory.

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:50.665
Now of course, that's no
good because if you're not

00:14:50.665 --> 00:14:52.040
using your GPU,
then you're going

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.910
to be orders of
magnitude too slow.

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:57.440
So you need to explicitly
say in PyTorch that you

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.340
need to move it to the GPU.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:03.920
And this is it's
actually just to make

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:07.410
it very clear in pictures,
there's a CPU, it has RAM,

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:12.120
and that has to be
moved over to the GPU.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:18.830
There's a data transfer,
which takes some work,

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:20.420
takes some time.

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:24.150
So whenever you have
a tensor in PyTorch,

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:25.950
you should always
keep in your mind.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.420
Where is this residing.

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.000
Because just looking
at the variable

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:30.167
or just looking at the code.

00:15:30.167 --> 00:15:31.190
You can't always tell.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:35.350
And if you want to be careful
about computation and data

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:39.310
movement, you have to
really know where it is.

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.950
You can probably do
things like assert

00:15:41.950 --> 00:15:46.420
where it is in various places
in the code just to document

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:48.370
or be sure.

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:55.760
OK, so let's look at
what hardware we have.

00:15:55.760 --> 00:15:59.530
So we have, in this
case, we have one GPU.

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:02.230
This was run on
the H100 clusters

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:04.190
that you guys have access to.

00:16:04.190 --> 00:16:11.320
And this GPU is
H100 80 gigabytes

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:14.830
of high bandwidth memory.

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:18.790
And there's it gives you
the cache size and so on.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:27.640
So, if you have remember
the x is on CPU,

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.360
you can move it just
by specifying 2, which

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.300
is a general PyTorch function.

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:36.470
You can also create a
tensor directly on the GPU,

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.370
so you don't have
to move it at all.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:44.200
And if everything goes well, I'm
looking at the memory allocated

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:45.940
before and after.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:55.510
The difference should be exactly
2 by 32 by 32 matrices of 4

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:56.830
by floats.

00:16:56.830 --> 00:17:01.360
So it's 8192.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.200
So this is just a sanity
check that the code

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:09.910
is doing what is advertised.

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:14.319
So now you have your
answers on the GPU.

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.250
What do you do?

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:18.940
So there's many
operations that you'll

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.099
be needing for assignment
one, and in general

00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:23.920
to do any deep
learning application.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:27.569
And most tensors you just
create by performing operations

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:29.340
on other tensors,
and each operations

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:32.050
has some memory and
compute footprint.

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:36.030
So let's make sure
we understand that.

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:41.520
So first of all, what is
actually a tensor in PyTorch.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:43.840
Tensors are a
mathematical object.

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:47.280
In PyTorch, they're
actually pointers

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:50.160
into some allocated memory.

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:54.925
So if you have,
let's say, a matrix 4

00:17:54.925 --> 00:18:01.380
by 4 matrix, what it actually
looks like is a long array.

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:04.410
And what the tensor
has is metadata

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:08.050
that specifies how to get
to address into that array.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:13.230
And the metadata is going to be,
two numbers a stride for each

00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:18.270
or actually one number per
dimension of the tensor.

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:23.160
In this case, because
there's two dimensions,

00:18:23.160 --> 00:18:25.710
stride zero and stride one.

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:31.990
Stride zero specifies if
you were in dimension zero,

00:18:31.990 --> 00:18:36.760
to get to the next row
to increment that index,

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.290
how many do you have to skip?

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:41.560
And so going down the
rows you skip four.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:44.680
So stride zero is four.

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:49.480
And to go to the next
column, you skip one.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:53.010
So stride one is one.

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:58.620
So with that, you find an
element let's say 1, 2.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:02.640
It's simply just multiply
the indexes by the stride

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:07.500
and you get to your
index which is six here.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:11.050
So that would be here or here.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:17.650
So that's basically what's going
underneath the hood for tensors.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:24.800
So this is relevant because you
can have multiple tensors that

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:25.910
use the same storage.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:27.410
And this is useful
because you don't

00:19:27.410 --> 00:19:30.180
want to copy the tensor
all over the place.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.920
So imagine you have
a 2 by 3 matrix here.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:38.113
Many operations don't
actually create a new tensor,

00:19:38.113 --> 00:19:39.530
they just create
a different view.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:41.660
And it doesn't
make a copy, so you

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:47.930
have to make sure that, if
you start mutating one tensor

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:50.900
it's going to cause the
other one to mutate.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:58.590
So for example, if
you just get row 0--

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:02.180
So remember y is this tensor--

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:09.960
sorry x is 123456, and y is x0
which is just the first row OK,

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:14.030
and you can double check there's
this function in row that

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:16.670
says if you look at
the underlying storage

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:19.770
whether these two tensors have
the same storage or not, OK.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:23.340
So this definitely
doesn't copy the tensor,

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.060
it just creates a view.

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:29.150
You can get column 1.

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:33.140
This also doesn't
copy the tensor.

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:35.420
Oops I don't need to do that.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:38.690
You can call a view function,
which can take any tensor

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:47.720
and look at it in terms of
different dimensions, 2 by 3.

00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:50.630
Actually this should be maybe
the other way around as a 3

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:53.300
by 2 tensor.

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:58.430
So that also doesn't
change, do any copying.

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:03.290
You can transpose,
that also doesn't copy.

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:09.260
And then, like I said, if you
start mutating x then y actually

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:12.470
gets mutated as well
because x and y are just

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:15.560
pointers into the same
underlying storage.

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:20.900
So one thing that you
have to be careful of

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:24.240
is that some views
are contiguous,

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.220
which means that if you
run through the tensor,

00:21:26.220 --> 00:21:32.270
it's like just going through
this array in your storage,

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.020
but some are not.

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:38.150
So in particular, if
you transpose it, now

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:40.080
what does it mean when
you're transposing it?

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.420
You're going down now.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:45.330
So if you imagine going
through the tensor,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.890
you're skipping around.

00:21:46.890 --> 00:21:50.150
And if you have a
non-contiguous tensor,

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:54.060
then if you try to further
view it in a different way,

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:57.050
then this is not going to work.

00:21:57.050 --> 00:22:01.700
So in some cases, if you
have a non-contiguous tensor

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:03.330
you can make it
contiguous first.

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:06.800
And then you can apply
whatever viewing operation

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:08.630
you want to it.

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:12.860
And then in this
case x and y do not

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:16.900
have the same storage because
contiguous in this case

00:22:16.900 --> 00:22:19.900
makes a copy.

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:26.950
So this is just ways of
slicing and dicing a tensor.

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:30.110
Views are free so
feel free to use them.

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:33.730
Define different variables
to make it easier

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:36.850
to read your code
because they're not

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:39.280
allocating any memory.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:42.700
But remember that
contiguous or reshape

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:46.120
which is basically
contiguous.view

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:52.880
can create a copy, and so just
be careful what you're doing OK.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.825
questions before moving on?

00:22:59.410 --> 00:23:00.320
All right.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:04.690
So hopefully a lot of this
will be review for those of you

00:23:04.690 --> 00:23:07.730
who have done a lot
of PyTorch before,

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:10.930
but it's helpful to just do
it systematically and make

00:23:10.930 --> 00:23:12.230
sure we're on the same page.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:18.280
So here are some operations
that do create new tensors

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:21.130
and in particular
element wise operations

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:22.940
all create new
tensors, obviously,

00:23:22.940 --> 00:23:26.770
because you need somewhere
else to store the new value.

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:32.380
There's a triangular u is
also an element operation that

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:35.830
comes in handy when you want
to create a causal attention

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:39.970
mask, which you'll need
for your assignment,

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:43.573
but nothing is that
interesting here.

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:49.100
So let's talk about matmuls.

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:51.220
So the bread and
butter of deep learning

00:23:51.220 --> 00:23:53.060
is matrix multiplications.

00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:55.820
And I'm sure all of you have
done a matrix multiplication,

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.430
but just in case
this is what it looks

00:23:57.430 --> 00:24:01.700
like, you take 16 by 32
times a 32 by 2 matrix,

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:04.630
you get a 16 by 2 matrix.

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:13.260
But in general when we do our
machine learning application,

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:16.930
all operations are you
want to do in a batch.

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:18.820
And in the case of
language models,

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:21.720
this usually means for
every example in a batch

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:25.200
and for every sequence in a
batch, you want to do something.

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:29.395
So generally what you're going
to have instead of just a matrix

00:24:29.395 --> 00:24:31.770
is you're going to have a
tensor where the dimensions are

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:33.580
typically batch sequence.

00:24:33.580 --> 00:24:36.910
And then whatever thing you're
trying to do, in this case,

00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:42.000
it's a matrix for every
token in your data set.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:47.710
And so PyTorch is nice enough
to make this work well for you.

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:52.870
So when you take this
four dimensional tensor,

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:57.000
and this matrix, what
actually ends up happening

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:01.030
is that for every batch,
every example and every token,

00:25:01.030 --> 00:25:04.920
you're multiplying
these two matrices.

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:09.450
And then the result is that
you get your resulting matrix

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:13.080
for each of the
first two elements.

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.060
There's nothing fancy
going on, but this

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:20.315
is just a pattern that I think
is helpful to think about.

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:28.290
So I'm going to take a
little bit of a digression

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:31.470
and talk about inops.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:35.740
And so the motivation for
inops is the following.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:41.290
So normally in PyTorch
you define some tensors.

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:43.300
And then you see
stuff like this,

00:25:43.300 --> 00:25:47.680
where you take x and multiply
by y transpose minus 2 minus 1.

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:52.830
And you look at this and you
say, OK, what is minus 2?

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:55.540
Well, I think that's
this sequence.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:57.570
And then minus 1 is this
hidden because you're

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.040
indexing backwards.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:00.810
And it's really
easy to mess this up

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:06.240
because if you look at your code
and you see minus 1 minus 2,

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:08.970
if you're good, you write
a bunch of comments.

00:26:08.970 --> 00:26:12.080
But then the comments can
get out of date with the code

00:26:12.080 --> 00:26:16.110
and then you have a
bad time debugging.

00:26:16.110 --> 00:26:20.460
So the solution is
to use Einops here.

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:24.590
So this is inspired by
Einstein summation notation.

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:26.330
And the idea is that
we're just going

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:32.990
to name all the dimensions
instead of relying on indices

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:34.190
essentially.

00:26:34.190 --> 00:26:36.720
So there's a library
called Jax typing,

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:44.120
which is helpful
as a way to specify

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.530
the dimensions in the types.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:51.000
So normally in PyTorch you would
just define, write your code,

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:52.940
and then you would
comment Oh, here's

00:26:52.940 --> 00:26:54.180
what the dimensions would be.

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:56.810
So if you use Jax
typing then you

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:59.000
have this notation
where as a string

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:01.440
you just write down
what the dimensions are.

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:06.770
So this is a slightly more
natural way of documenting.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.960
Now notice that
there's no enforcement

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:12.200
here because PyTorch
types are of a little bit

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:14.940
of a liam in PyTorch.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:15.680
So--

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:16.700
It can be enforced?

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.050
You can use a checker?

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:19.550
Yeah.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:27.480
You can write a check, but
not by default, yeah OK,

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.840
so let's look at the einsum.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:34.760
So einsum is basically matrix
multiplication on steroids

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:37.010
with good bookkeeping.

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.270
So here's our example.

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.980
Here we have x which is-- let's
just think about this as you

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.040
have a batch dimension.

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:47.610
You have a sequence dimension,
and you have four hit ends,

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:50.630
and y is the same size.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:54.380
You originally had
to do this thing.

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:58.760
And now what you do
instead is you basically

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:04.490
write down the dimensions, names
of the dimensions of the two

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:05.160
tensors.

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:08.520
So batch sequence 1 hidden,
batch sequence 2 hidden,

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:11.810
and you just write
what your dimension

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.760
should appear in the output.

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:16.790
So I write batch here
because I just want

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:20.940
to basically carry that over.

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:24.710
And then I write seq1
and seq2, and notice

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:26.550
that I don't write hidden.

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:28.790
And any dimension that is
not named in the output

00:28:28.790 --> 00:28:30.000
is just summed over.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:36.110
And any dimension that is
named is just iterated over.

00:28:36.110 --> 00:28:43.290
So once you get used to this
is actually very, very helpful.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.690
I mean, if you're seeing
this for the first time,

00:28:45.690 --> 00:28:48.600
it might seem a bit strange
and long, but trust me,

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:51.800
once you get used to it, it'll
be better than doing minus 2

00:28:51.800 --> 00:28:53.900
minus 1.

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:58.800
If you're a little bit,
slicker, you can use dot dot,

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:01.770
dot to represent broadcasting
over any number of dimensions.

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:06.080
So in this case, instead
of writing batch,

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:09.220
I can just write dot dot dot,
and this would handle the case

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:11.500
where instead of
maybe batch I have

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:17.560
batch 1 batch 2 or some other
arbitrary long sequence.

00:29:17.560 --> 00:29:18.680
Yeah, question?

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:20.900
Does Torch compile this?

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:25.930
Is it guaranteed to compile
to a condition, I guess?

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:28.540
So the question is it
guaranteed to compile

00:29:28.540 --> 00:29:32.125
to something efficient?

00:29:32.125 --> 00:29:35.140
I think the short answer is yes.

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.720
I don't know if you any nuances.

00:29:37.720 --> 00:29:39.490
Oh, to figure out the
best way to reduce,

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:42.850
the best order of dimensions
to reduce, and then use that.

00:29:42.850 --> 00:29:44.480
If you're using
within Torch compile,

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:46.060
it'll only do that
one time and then

00:29:46.060 --> 00:29:48.190
reuse the same implementation
over and over again.

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:51.010
It's usually better than
anything designed by hand?

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.535
Yeah, OK.

00:29:58.150 --> 00:30:00.110
So let's look at reduce.

00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:01.870
So reduce operates
on one tensor,

00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:06.220
and it basically aggregates
some dimension or dimensions

00:30:06.220 --> 00:30:06.980
of the tensor.

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.490
So you have this
tensor, before you

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:13.760
would write mean to sum
over the final dimension.

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:18.220
And now you basically
say actually, OK, so this

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:19.490
replaces with sum.

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:25.570
So reduce and again you
say hidden and hidden

00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:30.100
has disappeared, so which
means that you are aggregating

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:34.900
over that dimension OK, so
you can check that this indeed

00:30:34.900 --> 00:30:36.165
works over here.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:44.470
So maybe one final
example of this

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:50.350
is sometimes tensor
1 dimensio, actually

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:52.420
represents multiple
dimensions, and you

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:55.210
want to unpack that and
operate over one of them

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:56.420
and pack it back.

00:30:56.420 --> 00:31:01.050
So in this case, let's say
you have batch sequence

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:05.100
and then this eight
dimensional vector is actually

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:07.770
a flattened representation
of number of heads

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.560
times some hidden dimension

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:13.320
OK, so and then you
have a weight vector

00:31:13.320 --> 00:31:17.470
that needs to operate on
that hidden dimension.

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:22.470
So you can do this very
elegantly using einops

00:31:22.470 --> 00:31:26.230
by calling rearrange.

00:31:26.230 --> 00:31:29.110
And this basically,
you can think about it,

00:31:29.110 --> 00:31:34.050
we saw view before
it's a fancier

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:36.180
version, which basically
looks at the same data

00:31:36.180 --> 00:31:39.780
but differently.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:43.860
So here it basically says
this dimension is actually

00:31:43.860 --> 00:31:46.170
heads in hidden1, I'm
going to explode that

00:31:46.170 --> 00:31:49.410
into two dimensions.

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:54.420
And you have to specify
the number of heads

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:59.500
here because there's multiple
ways to split a number into two.

00:31:59.500 --> 00:32:02.860
Let's see, this might
be a little bit long.

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:06.700
OK, maybe it's not worth
looking at right now.

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:13.180
And given that x you can
perform your transformation

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:15.190
using einsum.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:19.090
So this is something
hidden1 which

00:32:19.090 --> 00:32:20.900
corresponds to x
and then hidden1,

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:23.710
hidden2 which corresponds
to w, and that

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:27.580
gives you something hidden2.

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:32.780
OK, and then you
can rearrange back.

00:32:32.780 --> 00:32:36.080
So this is just the
inverse of breaking up.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:39.760
So you have your two dimensions
and you group it into one.

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:41.800
So that's just a
flattening operation

00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:47.280
that's with everything all the
other dimensions left alone.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:55.670
So there is a tutorial
for this that I

00:32:55.670 --> 00:32:59.970
would recommend you go through
and it gives you a bit more.

00:32:59.970 --> 00:33:02.540
You don't have to use this
because you're building it

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:04.800
from scratch, so you can
do anything you want,

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:07.760
but in the assignment 1,
we do give you guidance

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:10.540
and it's something
probably to invest in.

00:33:15.916 --> 00:33:24.860
OK, so now let's talk
about computation

00:33:24.860 --> 00:33:27.390
no cost, of tensor operations.

00:33:27.390 --> 00:33:31.520
So we introduce a
bunch of operations,

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:33.720
and how much do they cost?

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:40.670
So a floating point operation
is any operation floating point

00:33:40.670 --> 00:33:43.170
like addition or multiplication.

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:47.510
These are them, and
these are the main ones

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:52.640
that are going to I think
matter in terms of FLOP count.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:57.660
One thing that is a pet peeve of
mine is that when you say FLOPs,

00:33:57.660 --> 00:33:59.490
it's actually unclear
what you mean.

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:02.640
So you could mean FLOPs
with a lowercase s,

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:06.210
which stands for number
of floating operations.

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:09.420
This measures amount of
computation that you've done.

00:34:09.420 --> 00:34:14.030
Or you could mean FLOPS also
written with an uppercase S

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:16.080
which means floating
points per second,

00:34:16.080 --> 00:34:19.260
which is used to measure
the speed of hardware.

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:24.830
So we're not going to in this
class use uppercase S because I

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:29.420
find that very confusing, and
just write /s to denote that

00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:33.812
this is floating
point per second, OK?

00:34:33.812 --> 00:34:37.760
OK, so just to give you
some intuition about FLOPs,

00:34:37.760 --> 00:34:47.100
GPT 3 took about 323 FLOPs, GPT
4 was 2025 FLOPs speculation.

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:50.600
And there was a US executive
order that any foundation model

00:34:50.600 --> 00:34:54.500
with over 126 FLOPs had to
be reported to government,

00:34:54.500 --> 00:35:02.900
which now has been revoked, but
the EU still has something that

00:35:02.900 --> 00:35:09.320
hasn't-- the EU AI act, which is
1e25 which hasn't been revoked.

00:35:09.320 --> 00:35:15.650
So some intuitions, A100
has a peak performance

00:35:15.650 --> 00:35:20.630
of 312 teraFLOP per second.

00:35:20.630 --> 00:35:26.900
And H100 has a peak
performance of 1979 teraFLOP

00:35:26.900 --> 00:35:32.420
per second with sparsity and
approximately 50% without.

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:38.510
And if you look the NVIDIA has
these specification sheets.

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:42.320
So you can see that the
FLOPs actually depends

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:44.430
on what you're trying to do.

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:48.880
So if you're using
BF or FP32, it's

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:51.280
actually really, really bad.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.870
If you run FP32 on H100,
you're not getting--

00:35:55.870 --> 00:36:02.830
it's orders of magnitude worse
than if you're doing FP16,

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:05.780
and if you're willing
to go down to FP8,

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:07.580
then it can be even faster.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:10.762
And for my first read
that I didn't realize,

00:36:10.762 --> 00:36:13.220
but there's an asterisk here,
and this means with sparsity.

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:18.280
So usually in a lot of the
matrices we have in this class

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:20.060
are dense, so you don't
actually get this,

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:21.790
you get something like--

00:36:21.790 --> 00:36:23.780
It's exactly half that number.

00:36:23.780 --> 00:36:25.275
Exactly half, OK.

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:36.590
So now you can do a back
of envelope calculations.

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:43.870
Eight H100s for two weeks is
just 8 times the number of FLOPs

00:36:43.870 --> 00:36:47.950
per second times the number
of seconds in a week.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:50.540
Actually, this
might be one week.

00:36:50.540 --> 00:36:57.400
So that's one week and
that's 4.7 times e to the 21

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.260
which is some number.

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:05.110
And you can contextualize the
FLOP counts with the other model

00:37:05.110 --> 00:37:07.840
counts, yeah.

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:09.040
Absolutely.

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:11.420
So that means if-- so
what is sparsity mean?

00:37:11.420 --> 00:37:14.050
That means if your
matrices are sparse.

00:37:14.050 --> 00:37:16.130
Is it specific for
structured sparsity?

00:37:16.130 --> 00:37:19.480
It's like two out of four
elements in each group of four

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:21.370
elements is 0.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:26.200
That's the only case where
you get that, no one uses it.

00:37:26.200 --> 00:37:29.360
Yeah, a marketing
department uses it.

00:37:32.881 --> 00:37:37.502
OK, so let's go through
a simple example.

00:37:37.502 --> 00:37:39.710
So remember we're not going
to touch the transformer,

00:37:39.710 --> 00:37:42.640
but I think even a linear model
gives us a lot of the building

00:37:42.640 --> 00:37:44.030
blocks, and intuitions.

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:46.990
So suppose we have end points.

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:50.220
Each point is D dimensional,
and the linear model

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:53.700
is just going to map each
D dimensional vector to a K

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:55.560
dimensional vector.

00:37:55.560 --> 00:38:00.360
So let's set some
number of points, is B

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:05.040
dimension is D K is
the number of outputs.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:08.580
And let's create
our data matrix x.

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:14.460
Our weight matrix w,
and the linear model

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:16.030
is just a map model.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:20.290
So nothing too
interesting going on,

00:38:20.290 --> 00:38:28.350
and the question is how
many FLOPs was that?

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:33.820
And the way you look
at this is you say,

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:38.310
well when you do the
matrix multiplication,

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:42.150
basically for every
i, j, k triple,

00:38:42.150 --> 00:38:46.110
I have to multiply
two numbers together,

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:52.350
And I also have to add
that number to the total.

00:38:52.350 --> 00:38:59.160
OK, so the answer is 2
times basically the product

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:00.820
of all the dimensions involved.

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:03.480
So the left dimension,
the middle dimension

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:07.250
and the right dimension.

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:10.450
So this is something that
you should just remember.

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:12.250
If you're doing a
matrix multiplication,

00:39:12.250 --> 00:39:15.930
the number of FLOPs
is 2 times the product

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:17.066
of the three dimensions.

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:23.580
OK, so the flops
of other operations

00:39:23.580 --> 00:39:31.110
are usually linear in the
size of the matrix or tensor.

00:39:31.110 --> 00:39:32.880
And in general,
no other operation

00:39:32.880 --> 00:39:34.350
you'll encounter
in deep learning

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:36.870
is expensive as
matrix multiplication

00:39:36.870 --> 00:39:39.100
for large enough matrices.

00:39:39.100 --> 00:39:42.090
So this is why I think
a lot of the napkin math

00:39:42.090 --> 00:39:44.490
is very simple
because we're only

00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:48.090
looking at the matrix
multiplications that

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.390
are performed by the model.

00:39:51.390 --> 00:39:53.070
Now of course, there
are regimes where

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:55.810
if your matrices
are small enough,

00:39:55.810 --> 00:39:58.000
then the cost of other
things starts to dominate.

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:00.390
But generally, that's
not a good regime

00:40:00.390 --> 00:40:02.670
you want to be in because
the hardware is designed

00:40:02.670 --> 00:40:08.320
for big matrix multiplication,
so it's a little bit circular,

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:13.170
but we end up in this regime
where we only consider models

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:19.410
where the map models
are the dominant cost.

00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:24.930
Any questions about this
number, 2 times the product

00:40:24.930 --> 00:40:26.110
of the three dimensions.

00:40:26.110 --> 00:40:28.050
This is just a useful thing.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:30.330
Should the algorithm of
matrix multiplication always

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:35.040
be the same because the chip
might have optimized it?

00:40:35.040 --> 00:40:39.020
Always only for the same.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:43.490
Yeah, so the question
is essentially

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:48.620
does this depend on the matrix
multiplication algorithm?

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:54.470
In general, I guess we'll
look at this the next week

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:56.370
or the week after when
we look at kernels.

00:40:56.370 --> 00:40:59.180
I mean actually there's a
lot of optimization that

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:01.460
goes underneath under
the hood when it comes

00:41:01.460 --> 00:41:04.340
to matrix multiplications.

00:41:04.340 --> 00:41:06.320
And there's a lot
of specialization

00:41:06.320 --> 00:41:09.590
depending on the shape.

00:41:09.590 --> 00:41:17.360
I would say this is just a
crude estimate that is basically

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:18.900
the right order of magnitude.

00:41:22.120 --> 00:41:25.610
OK, so yeah?

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:28.220
Additions and multiplications
are considered equivalent?

00:41:28.220 --> 00:41:29.840
Yeah, additions
and multiplications

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:33.770
are considered equivalent.

00:41:33.770 --> 00:41:37.890
So one way I find helpful
to interpret this.

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:40.530
So at the end of the day, this
is just a matrix multiplication,

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:44.930
but I'm going to try to give a
little bit of meaning to this,

00:41:44.930 --> 00:41:47.900
which is why I've set up
this as a little toy machine

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:48.870
learning problem.

00:41:48.870 --> 00:41:53.430
So B really stands for
the number of data points,

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:56.910
and D K is the
number of parameters.

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:59.270
So for this particular
model, the number

00:41:59.270 --> 00:42:01.430
of flops that's required
for a forward pass

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:03.890
is 2 times the number
of tokens or number

00:42:03.890 --> 00:42:07.010
of data points times the
number of parameters.

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:10.490
OK, so this turns out
to actually generalize

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:11.580
to transformers.

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:16.550
There's an asterisk
there because there's

00:42:16.550 --> 00:42:19.080
the sequence length
and other stuff,

00:42:19.080 --> 00:42:23.180
but this is roughly
right if your sequence

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:24.580
length isn't too large.

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:34.880
OK, so now this
is just the number

00:42:34.880 --> 00:42:38.140
of floating point operations.

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:40.330
So how does this
actually translate

00:42:40.330 --> 00:42:43.600
to wall clock time, which
is presumably the thing you

00:42:43.600 --> 00:42:44.630
actually care about?

00:42:44.630 --> 00:42:47.290
How long do you have
to wait for your run?

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:50.330
So let's time this.

00:42:50.330 --> 00:43:00.400
So I have this function that is
just going to do it five times,

00:43:00.400 --> 00:43:04.690
and I'm going to perform the
matrix multiply operation.

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:08.140
We'll talk a little
bit later about this,

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:11.530
two weeks from now why
the other code is here.

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:15.170
But for now we get
an actual time,

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:20.740
so that matrix
took 0.16 seconds,

00:43:20.740 --> 00:43:25.990
and the actual FLOPs per second,
which is how many flops did it

00:43:25.990 --> 00:43:31.750
do per second is 5.4e13.

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:37.420
So now you can compare this
with the marketing materials,

00:43:37.420 --> 00:43:45.560
and for the A100 and H100, and
as we look at the spec sheet,

00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:48.830
the flops depends
on the data type.

00:43:48.830 --> 00:43:52.780
And we see that the
promise of flops

00:43:52.780 --> 00:44:01.180
per second, which for H100, for
I guess this is for float 32

00:44:01.180 --> 00:44:06.980
is 67 teraflops as we looked.

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:12.800
And so that is the number
of promised FLOPs per second

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:15.310
we had.

00:44:15.310 --> 00:44:19.510
And now if you look at the--

00:44:19.510 --> 00:44:22.630
there's a helpful notion
called model FLOPs

00:44:22.630 --> 00:44:26.320
utilization or MFU, which is
the actual number of flops

00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:28.960
divided by the promised FLOPs.

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:31.390
So you take the actual
number of FLOPs,

00:44:31.390 --> 00:44:35.530
remember, which is what
you actually witnessed,

00:44:35.530 --> 00:44:37.500
the number of floating
point operations

00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:43.170
that are useful for
your model, divided

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.080
by the actual time it took
divided by this promise

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:50.070
flops per second, which is
from the glossy brochure.

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:55.170
You can get a MFU of 0.8.

00:44:55.170 --> 00:45:00.330
OK, so usually you see people
talking about their MFUs,

00:45:00.330 --> 00:45:04.980
and something greater
than 0.5 is usually

00:45:04.980 --> 00:45:06.610
considered to be good.

00:45:06.610 --> 00:45:11.110
And if you're 5% MFU that's
considered to be really bad.

00:45:11.110 --> 00:45:18.720
Usually it can't get
that close to 90 or 100

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:23.110
because this is ignoring all
communication and overhead,

00:45:23.110 --> 00:45:28.260
it's just the literal
computation of the FLOPs.

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:32.160
And usually MFU is much higher
if the matrix multiplication

00:45:32.160 --> 00:45:35.190
is dominate.

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:41.010
So that's MFU, any
questions about this, yeah?

00:45:41.010 --> 00:45:43.980
You're using the
promise flop per sec,

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:46.320
not considering the sparsity.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:52.230
So this promise flop per sec is
not considering this mismatch,

00:45:52.230 --> 00:45:58.140
yeah A note is like
this is actually--

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:03.460
there's also something called
hardware to FLOPs utilization,

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:13.800
and the motivation here is that
we're trying to look at the--

00:46:13.800 --> 00:46:15.930
it's called model
because we're looking

00:46:15.930 --> 00:46:19.230
at the number of effective
useful operations

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:23.790
that the model is
performing, and so it's

00:46:23.790 --> 00:46:25.330
a way of standardizing.

00:46:25.330 --> 00:46:27.300
It's not the actual
number of flops

00:46:27.300 --> 00:46:31.650
that are done because you
could have optimization

00:46:31.650 --> 00:46:33.870
in your code that
cache a few things

00:46:33.870 --> 00:46:40.380
or redo recomputation of some
things, and in some sense

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:42.190
you're still computing
the same model.

00:46:42.190 --> 00:46:44.940
So what matters is
that this is trying

00:46:44.940 --> 00:46:46.740
to look at the model
complexity, and you

00:46:46.740 --> 00:46:48.600
shouldn't be penalized
just because you

00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:51.390
were clever in your
MFU if you were clever

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:57.090
and you didn't actually do the
FLOPs, but you said you did.

00:46:57.090 --> 00:46:59.950
OK, so you can also
do the same with bf16.

00:47:02.970 --> 00:47:10.090
Oops, and here we
see that for a bf

00:47:10.090 --> 00:47:19.240
the time is actually much
better, so 0.03 instead of 0.16.

00:47:19.240 --> 00:47:24.000
So the actual FLOPs
per second is higher.

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:26.100
Even accounting
for its sparsity,

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:29.610
the promise FLOPs
is still quite high,

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:32.960
so the MFU is actually
lower for bf16.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:43.430
This is maybe surprisingly low,
but sometimes the promise FLOPs

00:47:43.430 --> 00:47:45.024
is a bit of optimistic.

00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:52.190
So always benchmark your
code, and don't just

00:47:52.190 --> 00:47:55.940
assume that you're going to get
certain levels of performance.

00:47:55.940 --> 00:48:02.330
So just to summarize,
matrix multiplications

00:48:02.330 --> 00:48:06.920
dominate the compute, and
the general rule of thumb

00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:12.920
is that it's 2 times the product
of the dimensions, FLOPs.

00:48:12.920 --> 00:48:17.930
The FLOPs per second,
Floating Points per second

00:48:17.930 --> 00:48:22.770
depends on the hardware
and also the data type.

00:48:22.770 --> 00:48:26.130
So the fancier the hardware
you have, the higher it is.

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:32.030
The smaller the data type,
usually the faster it is.

00:48:32.030 --> 00:48:36.110
And MFU is a useful
notion to look

00:48:36.110 --> 00:48:45.080
at how well you're essentially
squeezing your hardware, yeah?

00:48:45.080 --> 00:48:48.570
I've heard that often in order
to get the maximum utilization,

00:48:48.570 --> 00:48:51.390
you want to use these
tensor cores on the machine.

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:54.800
And so this PyTorch by
default use these tensor cores

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:58.530
and are these calculations
accounting for that?

00:48:58.530 --> 00:48:59.030
Yeah,

00:48:59.030 --> 00:49:02.310
So the question is, what
about those tensor cores?

00:49:02.310 --> 00:49:07.940
So if you go to this
spec sheet, you'll

00:49:07.940 --> 00:49:13.800
see that these are all
on the tensor core.

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:17.630
So the tensor core is basically
a specialized hardware

00:49:17.630 --> 00:49:18.883
to do matmuls.

00:49:26.860 --> 00:49:28.850
So by default it should use it.

00:49:28.850 --> 00:49:31.730
And especially if you're
using PyTorch in your compile,

00:49:31.730 --> 00:49:36.795
it will generate the code that
will use the hardware properly.

00:49:43.900 --> 00:49:48.530
So let's talk a little
bit about gradients.

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:51.640
And the reason is
that we've only

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:55.870
looked at matrix multiplication
or in other words, basically

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:59.740
feedforward forward passes
and the number of flops,

00:49:59.740 --> 00:50:01.300
but there's also
a computation that

00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:03.160
comes from computing
gradients, and we want

00:50:03.160 --> 00:50:07.390
to track down how much that is.

00:50:07.390 --> 00:50:12.410
OK, so just to consider
a simple example,

00:50:12.410 --> 00:50:15.910
a simple linear
model where you take

00:50:15.910 --> 00:50:21.190
the prediction of a
linear model and you

00:50:21.190 --> 00:50:23.420
look at the MSC with
respect to five.

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:25.900
So not a very
interesting loss, but I

00:50:25.900 --> 00:50:31.180
think it's illustrative for
looking at the gradients.

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:31.730
OK.

00:50:31.730 --> 00:50:34.160
So remember, in the forward
pass you have your X,

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:38.950
you have your W, which
you want to compute

00:50:38.950 --> 00:50:40.570
the gradient with
respect to-- you

00:50:40.570 --> 00:50:44.660
make a prediction by
taking the linear product.

00:50:44.660 --> 00:50:48.430
And then you have your loss.

00:50:48.430 --> 00:50:49.130
OK.

00:50:49.130 --> 00:50:53.510
And in the backward pass,
you just call loss backwards.

00:50:53.510 --> 00:50:58.390
And in this case,
the gradient, which

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:02.155
is this variable
attached to the tensor

00:51:02.155 --> 00:51:06.230
turns out to be what you want.

00:51:06.230 --> 00:51:12.820
So everyone has done,
gradients in PyTorch before.

00:51:12.820 --> 00:51:16.870
So let's look at how
many flops are required

00:51:16.870 --> 00:51:20.562
for computing gradients.

00:51:20.562 --> 00:51:21.062
OK.

00:51:21.062 --> 00:51:29.800
So let's look at a slightly
more complicated model.

00:51:29.800 --> 00:51:34.860
So now, it's a
two-layer linear model,

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:43.020
where you have x which is B by
D, times W1 which is D by D.

00:51:43.020 --> 00:51:44.980
So that's the first layer.

00:51:44.980 --> 00:51:49.290
And then, you take your
hidden activations H1

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:53.110
and you pass it through
another linear layer W2.

00:51:53.110 --> 00:51:56.610
And to get a K dimensional
vector and you do some--

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:57.575
compute some loss.

00:52:00.750 --> 00:52:03.340
So this is a two
layer linear network.

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:06.090
And just as a
review, if you look

00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:10.170
at the number of forward
flops, what you had to do

00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:13.110
was you have to multiply--

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:14.650
look at W1.

00:52:14.650 --> 00:52:21.940
You have to multiply x by
W1 and add it to your H1,

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:25.230
and you have to take
H1 and W2 and you

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:29.370
have to add it to your H2.

00:52:29.370 --> 00:52:31.860
So the total number
of flops again,

00:52:31.860 --> 00:52:36.330
is 2 times the product of all
the dimensions in your matmul

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:39.570
plus 2 times the product
dimensions of your matmul

00:52:39.570 --> 00:52:41.400
for the second matrix.

00:52:41.400 --> 00:52:44.310
In other words, 2 times the
total number of parameters,

00:52:44.310 --> 00:52:46.560
in this case.

00:52:46.560 --> 00:52:50.260
OK, so what about
the backward pass.

00:52:50.260 --> 00:52:54.960
So this part will be a
little bit more involved.

00:52:54.960 --> 00:53:00.730
So we can recall the Model
x to H1 to H2 and the loss.

00:53:00.730 --> 00:53:02.220
So in the backward
pass, you have

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:04.840
to compute a bunch of gradients.

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:06.780
And the gradients
that are relevant

00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:16.420
is you have to compute the
gradient with respect to H1, H2,

00:53:16.420 --> 00:53:21.420
W1 and W2 of the loss.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:24.670
So d loss each of
these variables.

00:53:24.670 --> 00:53:27.550
OK, so how long does it
take to compute that?

00:53:27.550 --> 00:53:31.360
Let's just look
at W2, for now OK.

00:53:31.360 --> 00:53:35.760
So the things that touch
W2, you can compute

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:38.970
by looking at the chain rule.

00:53:38.970 --> 00:53:43.180
So W2 grad.

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:50.410
So the gradient of
d loss, D W2, is

00:53:50.410 --> 00:53:59.880
you sum H1 times the gradient
of the loss with respect to H2.

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:01.095
OK.

00:54:01.095 --> 00:54:04.033
So that's just the
chain rule for W2.

00:54:07.830 --> 00:54:11.010
So all the gradients
are the same size

00:54:11.010 --> 00:54:15.560
as the underlying of vectors.

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:20.390
So this turns out to be--

00:54:20.390 --> 00:54:24.240
essentially, looks like
a matrix multiplication.

00:54:24.240 --> 00:54:27.860
And so the same
calculus holds, which

00:54:27.860 --> 00:54:32.840
is that it's 2 times the
number of the product of all

00:54:32.840 --> 00:54:38.070
the dimensions B
times D, times K. OK.

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:43.460
But this is only the
gradient with respect to W2.

00:54:43.460 --> 00:54:46.250
We also need to compute
the gradient with respect

00:54:46.250 --> 00:54:52.280
to H1, because we have to keep
on back propagating to W1 and so

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:52.940
on.

00:54:52.940 --> 00:55:07.790
So that is going to be the
product of W2 times H2.

00:55:07.790 --> 00:55:13.910
Sorry, I think this should
be a grad of H2, H2 grad.

00:55:13.910 --> 00:55:19.160
So that turns out to
also be essentially,

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:21.810
looks like the matrix
multiplication.

00:55:21.810 --> 00:55:26.960
And it's the same number
of flops for computing

00:55:26.960 --> 00:55:30.740
the gradient of H1.

00:55:30.740 --> 00:55:36.230
So when you add the two
so that's just for W2

00:55:36.230 --> 00:55:38.190
you do the same thing for W1.

00:55:38.190 --> 00:55:41.430
And that's which has
D times D parameters.

00:55:41.430 --> 00:55:46.070
And when you add
it all up, it's--

00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:51.230
so for this-- for W2 the
amount of computation

00:55:51.230 --> 00:55:54.870
was four times B,
times D, times K.

00:55:54.870 --> 00:56:01.940
And for W1 it's also 4
times B, times D, times D,

00:56:01.940 --> 00:56:09.520
because W1 is D by D. OK.

00:56:09.520 --> 00:56:12.890
So I know there's a
lot of symbols here.

00:56:12.890 --> 00:56:17.630
I'm going to try also to give
you a visual account for this.

00:56:17.630 --> 00:56:23.120
So this is from a blog post
that, I think, may work better.

00:56:23.120 --> 00:56:24.520
We'll see.

00:56:24.520 --> 00:56:26.870
OK, I have to wait for the
animation to loop back.

00:56:26.870 --> 00:56:30.040
So basically, this is one
layer of the neural net

00:56:30.040 --> 00:56:33.190
where it has the
hiddens and then

00:56:33.190 --> 00:56:35.510
the weights to the next layer.

00:56:35.510 --> 00:56:37.510
And so I have to--

00:56:37.510 --> 00:56:38.060
OK.

00:56:38.060 --> 00:56:40.065
Problem with this animation
is I have to wait.

00:56:40.065 --> 00:56:41.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:42.430
OK.

00:56:42.430 --> 00:56:44.210
Ready, set, OK.

00:56:44.210 --> 00:56:48.280
So first, I have to
multiply w and a,

00:56:48.280 --> 00:56:49.700
and I have to add it to this.

00:56:49.700 --> 00:56:50.690
That's a forward pass.

00:56:50.690 --> 00:56:52.720
And now, I'm going
to multiply these two

00:56:52.720 --> 00:56:55.040
and then add it to that.

00:56:55.040 --> 00:56:58.005
And I'm going to multiply
and then add it to that, OK.

00:56:58.005 --> 00:57:00.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:00.670 --> 00:57:01.420
Any questions?

00:57:01.420 --> 00:57:03.280
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:03.280 --> 00:57:06.340
I wish there's way
to slow this down.

00:57:06.340 --> 00:57:11.600
But the details-- maybe
I'll let you ruminate on,

00:57:11.600 --> 00:57:14.050
but the high level
is that there's

00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:17.045
two times the number of
parameters for the forward pass

00:57:17.045 --> 00:57:18.670
and four times the
number of parameters

00:57:18.670 --> 00:57:19.730
for the backward pass.

00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:24.230
And we can just work it out
via the chain rule here.

00:57:24.230 --> 00:57:24.970
Yeah.

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:27.630
For the homeworks, are
we also using the--

00:57:27.630 --> 00:57:31.160
you said some PyTorch
implementation as well as some--

00:57:31.160 --> 00:57:35.260
are we allowed to use
grad or we are doing the--

00:57:35.260 --> 00:57:38.980
entirely by hand
doing the gradient?

00:57:38.980 --> 00:57:41.090
So the question is,
in the homework,

00:57:41.090 --> 00:57:43.340
are you going to compute
gradients by hand,

00:57:43.340 --> 00:57:44.600
and the answer is no.

00:57:44.600 --> 00:57:47.210
You're going to just
use PyTorch gradient.

00:57:47.210 --> 00:57:52.245
This is just to break it down
so we can do the counting flops.

00:57:55.360 --> 00:57:58.140
Any questions about
this before I move on?

00:58:05.100 --> 00:58:05.650
OK.

00:58:05.650 --> 00:58:08.040
Just to summarize,
the forward pass

00:58:08.040 --> 00:58:12.900
is, for this particular model
is 2 times the number of data

00:58:12.900 --> 00:58:14.650
points, times the
number of parameters,

00:58:14.650 --> 00:58:17.340
and backward is 4 times
the number of data points,

00:58:17.340 --> 00:58:19.710
times the number of parameters,
which means that total,

00:58:19.710 --> 00:58:22.890
it's six times the number of
data points, times parameters.

00:58:22.890 --> 00:58:26.580
And that's explains
why there was

00:58:26.580 --> 00:58:30.900
that 6 in the beginning when I
asked the motivating question.

00:58:30.900 --> 00:58:36.090
So now, this is for a
simple linear model.

00:58:36.090 --> 00:58:40.410
But it turns out that many
models, this is basically,

00:58:40.410 --> 00:58:44.100
the bulk of the computation,
when essentially

00:58:44.100 --> 00:58:51.330
every computation you do touches
essentially new parameters,

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:52.710
roughly.

00:58:52.710 --> 00:58:56.017
And obviously,
this doesn't hold.

00:58:56.017 --> 00:58:57.600
You can find models
where this doesn't

00:58:57.600 --> 00:59:00.240
hold because you can have one
parameter through parameter

00:59:00.240 --> 00:59:03.010
sharing and have
a billion flops,

00:59:03.010 --> 00:59:06.500
but that's generally what,
not what models look like.

00:59:09.300 --> 00:59:16.140
OK, so let me move on.

00:59:16.140 --> 00:59:21.480
So far, I've basically finished
talking about the resource

00:59:21.480 --> 00:59:22.030
accounting.

00:59:22.030 --> 00:59:23.440
So we looked at tensors.

00:59:23.440 --> 00:59:25.360
We looked at some
computation on tensors.

00:59:25.360 --> 00:59:28.950
We looked at how
much tensors take

00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:32.730
to store and also how
many flops tensors

00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:36.180
take when you do various
operations on them.

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:40.560
Now, let's start building
up different models.

00:59:40.560 --> 00:59:44.940
I think this part isn't
necessarily going to be,

00:59:44.940 --> 00:59:49.750
that conceptually
interesting or challenging,

00:59:49.750 --> 00:59:53.400
but it's more for maybe
this completeness.

00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:59.190
So parameters in
PyTorch are stored

00:59:59.190 --> 01:00:04.320
is as these
nn.parameter objects.

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:08.820
Let's talk a little bit about
parameter initialization.

01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:19.010
So if you have, let's say,
a parameter that has--

01:00:19.010 --> 01:00:19.510
OK.

01:00:19.510 --> 01:00:22.620
So you generate a--

01:00:22.620 --> 01:00:23.350
sorry.

01:00:23.350 --> 01:00:25.590
Your w parameter is
an input dimension

01:00:25.590 --> 01:00:28.457
by hidden dimension matrix.

01:00:28.457 --> 01:00:30.040
You're still in the
linear model case.

01:00:30.040 --> 01:00:33.800
So let's just say an input and
let's feed it through the output

01:00:33.800 --> 01:00:34.300
OK.

01:00:34.300 --> 01:00:41.370
So Rand and unit
Gaussian seems innocuous.

01:00:41.370 --> 01:00:45.240
What happens when you do this is
that if you look at the output,

01:00:45.240 --> 01:00:48.070
you get some pretty
large numbers.

01:00:48.070 --> 01:00:51.660
And this is because
when you have

01:00:51.660 --> 01:00:56.160
the number of rows as
essentially the square root

01:00:56.160 --> 01:00:58.460
of the hidden dimension.

01:00:58.460 --> 01:01:03.120
And so when you have large
models this is going to blow up.

01:01:03.120 --> 01:01:08.210
And training can
be a very unstable.

01:01:08.210 --> 01:01:13.850
So typically, what you want
to do is initialize in a way

01:01:13.850 --> 01:01:17.420
that's invariant to
hidden, or at least

01:01:17.420 --> 01:01:20.220
when you're guaranteed that
it's not going to blow up.

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:24.680
And one simple way to do
this is just rescale by the 1

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:28.970
over square root of
number of inputs.

01:01:28.970 --> 01:01:34.640
So basically, let's redo this
w equals a parameter where

01:01:34.640 --> 01:01:40.160
I simply divide by the square
root of the input dimension.

01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:43.580
And then now, when you feed
it through the output now,

01:01:43.580 --> 01:01:46.430
you get things that
are stable around--

01:01:46.430 --> 01:01:51.445
this actually concentrate to
something like normal zero, one.

01:01:54.656 --> 01:01:58.760
So this is basically-- this has
been explored pretty extensively

01:01:58.760 --> 01:02:02.240
in deep learning literatures
known up to a constant,

01:02:02.240 --> 01:02:03.930
this is Xavier initialization.

01:02:03.930 --> 01:02:07.070
And typically, I gets
it's fairly common

01:02:07.070 --> 01:02:08.862
if you want to be extra safe.

01:02:08.862 --> 01:02:10.820
You don't trust the normal
because that doesn't

01:02:10.820 --> 01:02:12.900
have it has unbounded tails.

01:02:12.900 --> 01:02:14.992
And you just say I'm
going to truncate to -3,

01:02:14.992 --> 01:02:17.520
3 so I don't get
any large values,

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:21.275
and I don't want any
to mess with that.

01:02:21.275 --> 01:02:21.775
OK.

01:02:30.710 --> 01:02:35.720
So let's build just
a simple model.

01:02:35.720 --> 01:02:41.070
It's going to have D
dimensions and two layers.

01:02:41.070 --> 01:02:43.770
There's this-- I just made
up this name, cruncher,

01:02:43.770 --> 01:02:47.930
it's a custom model, which is
a deep linear network, which

01:02:47.930 --> 01:02:51.840
has n num layers layers.

01:02:51.840 --> 01:02:56.810
And each layer is
a linear model,

01:02:56.810 --> 01:03:00.020
which has essentially, it's
just a matrix multiplication.

01:03:04.780 --> 01:03:08.650
So the parameters
of this model looks

01:03:08.650 --> 01:03:17.830
like I have layers for--
the first layer, which

01:03:17.830 --> 01:03:19.900
is a D by D matrix.

01:03:19.900 --> 01:03:22.100
The second layer, which
is also D by D matrix.

01:03:22.100 --> 01:03:27.670
And then I have a
head or a final layer.

01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:33.820
So if I get the number of
parameters of this model

01:03:33.820 --> 01:03:40.000
then it's going to be D squared
plus D squared, plus D. So

01:03:40.000 --> 01:03:42.940
nothing too surprising there.

01:03:42.940 --> 01:03:45.250
And I'm going to
move it to the GPU

01:03:45.250 --> 01:03:48.230
because I want this to run fast.

01:03:48.230 --> 01:03:51.430
And I'm going to
generate some random data

01:03:51.430 --> 01:03:54.400
and feed it through the
data, and the forward pass

01:03:54.400 --> 01:03:58.660
is just going through
the layers and then,

01:03:58.660 --> 01:03:59.970
finally applying the head.

01:04:04.210 --> 01:04:07.930
So with that model
let's try to--

01:04:07.930 --> 01:04:11.440
I'm going to use this model
and do some stuff with it.

01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:14.800
But just one general digression.

01:04:14.800 --> 01:04:22.330
Randomness is something that
can be annoying in some cases,

01:04:22.330 --> 01:04:24.830
be it if you're trying to
reproduce a bug, for example.

01:04:24.830 --> 01:04:27.620
It shows up in many places
initialization, dropout,

01:04:27.620 --> 01:04:32.440
data ordering, and
just a best practices.

01:04:32.440 --> 01:04:37.150
We recommend you always
pass a fixed random seed.

01:04:37.150 --> 01:04:43.060
So you can reproduce your model,
or at least, as well as you can.

01:04:43.060 --> 01:04:46.750
And in particular, having
a different random seed

01:04:46.750 --> 01:04:49.920
for every source of
randomness is nice

01:04:49.920 --> 01:04:52.710
because then you can, for
example, fix the initialization

01:04:52.710 --> 01:04:56.670
or fix the data ordering,
but very other things.

01:04:56.670 --> 01:05:02.830
Determinism is your friend when
you're debugging and encode.

01:05:02.830 --> 01:05:05.040
Unfortunately,
there's many places

01:05:05.040 --> 01:05:11.040
where you can use randomness and
just be cognizant of which one

01:05:11.040 --> 01:05:11.680
you're using.

01:05:11.680 --> 01:05:14.370
And just if you want
to be safe, just

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:16.355
set the seed for all of them.

01:05:19.290 --> 01:05:21.450
Data loading.

01:05:21.450 --> 01:05:23.180
I guess I'll go
through this quickly.

01:05:26.250 --> 01:05:29.400
it'll be useful for
your assignment.

01:05:29.400 --> 01:05:32.910
So in language modeling,
data is typically

01:05:32.910 --> 01:05:34.390
just a sequence of integers.

01:05:34.390 --> 01:05:37.440
Because this is remember
output by the tokenizer.

01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:40.740
And you serialize them into--

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:44.220
you can serialize them
into numpy arrays.

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:51.840
And one thing that's maybe
useful is that you don't want

01:05:51.840 --> 01:05:54.630
to load all your data
into memory at once

01:05:54.630 --> 01:05:59.160
because for example,
the LLaMA data is 2.8TB,

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:03.810
but you can pretend to load it
by using this handy function

01:06:03.810 --> 01:06:08.790
called mmemap, which gives you
essentially a variable that is

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:10.390
mapped to a file.

01:06:10.390 --> 01:06:16.080
So when you try to access the
data, it actually on demand

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:17.970
loads the file.

01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:21.300
And then using that
you can create a data

01:06:21.300 --> 01:06:28.510
loader that sample of
data from your batch.

01:06:28.510 --> 01:06:32.820
So I'm going to skip over that,
just in the interest of time.

