01:06:50.007 --> 01:06:52.090
So of course, there's
stochastic gradient descent.

01:06:52.090 --> 01:06:53.980
You compute the
gradient of your batch,

01:06:53.980 --> 01:06:57.300
you take a step in that
direction, no questions asked.

01:06:57.300 --> 01:07:01.380
There's a idea called momentum
which dates back to Classic

01:07:01.380 --> 01:07:07.140
optimization, Nesterov , where
you have a running average

01:07:07.140 --> 01:07:09.670
of your gradients.

01:07:09.670 --> 01:07:13.110
And you update against the
running average instead

01:07:13.110 --> 01:07:16.110
of your instantaneous gradient.

01:07:16.110 --> 01:07:23.370
And then you have AdaGrad,
which you scale the gradients

01:07:23.370 --> 01:07:30.600
by the average over
the norms of your,

01:07:30.600 --> 01:07:34.450
or I guess not the norms,
the square of the gradients.

01:07:34.450 --> 01:07:39.390
You also have RMSProp, which is
an improved version of AdaGrad,

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:42.930
which uses an exponential
averaging rather than just

01:07:42.930 --> 01:07:44.460
a flat average.

01:07:44.460 --> 01:07:46.550
And then finally,
Adam, which appeared

01:07:46.550 --> 01:07:50.970
in 2014, which is essentially
combining RMSProp and momentum.

01:07:50.970 --> 01:07:56.240
So that's why you're maintaining
both your running average

01:07:56.240 --> 01:07:58.490
of your gradients, but
also running average

01:07:58.490 --> 01:08:00.260
of your gradient squared.

01:08:00.260 --> 01:08:06.330
So since you're going to
implement Adam in homework 1,

01:08:06.330 --> 01:08:07.380
I'm not going to do that.

01:08:07.380 --> 01:08:11.010
Instead, I'm going
to implement AdaGrad.

01:08:11.010 --> 01:08:17.870
So the way you implement
the optimizer in PyTorch

01:08:17.870 --> 01:08:20.250
is that you override
the optimizer class.

01:08:20.250 --> 01:08:27.560
And you have to, let's
see, maybe I'll and then

01:08:27.560 --> 01:08:30.529
I'll get to the implementation
once we step through it.

01:08:30.529 --> 01:08:37.069
So let's define some data,
compute the forward pass

01:08:37.069 --> 01:08:38.640
on the loss.

01:08:38.640 --> 01:08:42.189
And then you compute
the gradients,

01:08:42.189 --> 01:08:47.240
and then, when you
call optimizer.step,

01:08:47.240 --> 01:08:52.500
this is where the optimizer
actually is active.

01:08:52.500 --> 01:08:58.830
So what this looks like is,
your parameters are grouped by,

01:08:58.830 --> 01:09:02.060
for example, you have one for
the layer 0, layer 1, and then

01:09:02.060 --> 01:09:05.090
the final weights.

01:09:05.090 --> 01:09:13.490
And you can access a state which
is a dictionary from parameters

01:09:13.490 --> 01:09:17.069
to whatever you want to store.

01:09:17.069 --> 01:09:21.140
As the optimizer
state, the gradient

01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:23.660
of that parameter
you assume is already

01:09:23.660 --> 01:09:28.850
calculated by the backward pass.

01:09:28.850 --> 01:09:35.420
And now, you can do
things like in AdaGrad,

01:09:35.420 --> 01:09:39.450
you're storing the sum
of the gradient squared.

01:09:39.450 --> 01:09:44.410
So you can get that g2 variable
and you can update that

01:09:44.410 --> 01:09:46.189
based on the square
of the gradient.

01:09:46.189 --> 01:09:50.000
So this is the element wise
squaring of the gradient.

01:09:50.000 --> 01:09:52.750
And you put it back
into the state.

01:09:52.750 --> 01:09:55.450
So then your, obviously,
your optimizer

01:09:55.450 --> 01:09:58.400
is responsible for
updating the parameters.

01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:01.420
And this is just you
update the learning

01:10:01.420 --> 01:10:04.900
rate times the gradient,
divided by this scaling.

01:10:04.900 --> 01:10:11.080
So now, this state is kept over
across multiple invocations

01:10:11.080 --> 01:10:13.566
of the optimizer.

01:10:20.422 --> 01:10:23.350
And then at the end
of your optimizer step

01:10:23.350 --> 01:10:27.730
you can free up the
memory just to--

01:10:27.730 --> 01:10:30.250
which is, I think,
going to actually

01:10:30.250 --> 01:10:33.010
be more important
when you look when we

01:10:33.010 --> 01:10:35.440
talk about model parallelism.

01:10:35.440 --> 01:10:41.380
So let's talk about the memory
requirements of the optimizer

01:10:41.380 --> 01:10:41.930
states.

01:10:41.930 --> 01:10:44.300
And actually basically,
at this point, everything.

01:10:44.300 --> 01:10:50.680
So you need the number of
parameters in this model

01:10:50.680 --> 01:10:53.440
is D squared times
the number of layers

01:10:53.440 --> 01:10:56.800
plus D for the final head.

01:10:56.800 --> 01:10:57.300
OK.

01:10:59.830 --> 01:11:03.400
The number of
activations, so this

01:11:03.400 --> 01:11:05.800
is something we didn't
do before, but now,

01:11:05.800 --> 01:11:08.210
for this simple model,
it's fairly easy to do.

01:11:08.210 --> 01:11:14.200
It's just B times
D, times the number

01:11:14.200 --> 01:11:17.300
of layers you have for every
layer, for every data point,

01:11:17.300 --> 01:11:18.230
for every dimension.

01:11:18.230 --> 01:11:21.580
You have to hold
the activations.

01:11:21.580 --> 01:11:27.100
For the gradients, this is the
same as the number of parameters

01:11:27.100 --> 01:11:30.520
and the number of
optimizer states,

01:11:30.520 --> 01:11:34.360
for AdaGrad, it's
remember, we had

01:11:34.360 --> 01:11:39.640
to store the gradient squared.

01:11:39.640 --> 01:11:42.090
So that's another copy
of the parameters.

01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:48.330
So putting it all together,
we have the total memory

01:11:48.330 --> 01:11:55.080
is assuming FP32, which means
four bytes, times the number

01:11:55.080 --> 01:11:58.230
of parameters, number of
activations, number of gradients

01:11:58.230 --> 01:12:00.500
and number of optimizer states.

01:12:00.500 --> 01:12:01.000
OK.

01:12:01.000 --> 01:12:09.930
And that gives us some
number, which is 496 here.

01:12:09.930 --> 01:12:13.560
So this is a fairly
simple calculation,

01:12:13.560 --> 01:12:16.170
in the assignment
1, you're going

01:12:16.170 --> 01:12:17.790
to do this for the
transformer, which

01:12:17.790 --> 01:12:20.220
is a little bit more
involved because you have

01:12:20.220 --> 01:12:22.720
to-- there's not just
matrix multiplications,

01:12:22.720 --> 01:12:24.730
but there's many matrices.

01:12:24.730 --> 01:12:26.980
There is attention and there's
all these other things.

01:12:26.980 --> 01:12:31.780
But the general form of the
calculation is the same.

01:12:31.780 --> 01:12:34.470
You have parameters,
activations, gradients

01:12:34.470 --> 01:12:35.990
and optimizer states.

01:12:44.410 --> 01:12:48.690
And the flops required,
again, for this model

01:12:48.690 --> 01:12:52.230
is six times the
number of tokens

01:12:52.230 --> 01:12:55.480
or the number of data points,
times the number of parameters.

01:12:55.480 --> 01:13:00.750
And that's basically concludes
the resource accounting

01:13:00.750 --> 01:13:04.320
for this particular model.

01:13:04.320 --> 01:13:08.580
And if for reference, if you're
curious about working this out

01:13:08.580 --> 01:13:13.055
for transformers, you can
consult some of these articles.

01:13:15.861 --> 01:13:18.960
So in the remaining
time, I think

01:13:18.960 --> 01:13:22.200
maybe I'll pause for questions.

01:13:22.200 --> 01:13:24.820
And we talked about
building up the tensors.

01:13:24.820 --> 01:13:27.310
And then we built
a very small model.

01:13:27.310 --> 01:13:32.670
And we talked about
optimization and how much

01:13:32.670 --> 01:13:34.955
memory and how much
compute was required.

01:13:38.530 --> 01:13:39.030
Yeah.

01:13:39.030 --> 01:13:41.970
Do we really need
to store activation?

01:13:41.970 --> 01:13:45.070
So the question is, why do you
need to store the activations.

01:13:45.070 --> 01:13:47.740
So naively, you need to
store the activations

01:13:47.740 --> 01:13:52.660
because when you're
doing the backward pass,

01:13:52.660 --> 01:13:56.100
the gradients of let's
say the first layer depend

01:13:56.100 --> 01:13:57.010
on the activation.

01:13:57.010 --> 01:13:59.790
So the gradients of the Ith
layer depends on the activation

01:13:59.790 --> 01:14:01.650
there.

01:14:01.650 --> 01:14:04.620
Now, if you're
smarter, you don't

01:14:04.620 --> 01:14:06.390
have to store the
activations or you don't

01:14:06.390 --> 01:14:07.570
have to store all of them.

01:14:07.570 --> 01:14:09.520
You can recompute them.

01:14:09.520 --> 01:14:11.370
And that's something
a technique will

01:14:11.370 --> 01:14:14.405
called activation checkpointing,
which we can talk about later.

01:14:19.380 --> 01:14:22.290
So let's just do this quick--

01:14:22.290 --> 01:14:24.460
actually, there's
not much to say here.

01:14:24.460 --> 01:14:31.320
But here's your typical training
loop where you define the model,

01:14:31.320 --> 01:14:35.960
define the optimizer
and you get the data.

01:14:35.960 --> 01:14:43.070
Feed forward, backward and
take a step in parameter space.

01:14:43.070 --> 01:14:49.910
And I guess it'll be
more interesting--

01:14:49.910 --> 01:14:53.310
I guess next time I should
show like an actual 1D plot,

01:14:53.310 --> 01:14:56.125
which isn't available
on this version.

01:14:59.150 --> 01:15:04.640
So one note about checkpointing.

01:15:04.640 --> 01:15:07.790
So training language
models takes a long time

01:15:07.790 --> 01:15:11.190
and you certainly will
crash at some point.

01:15:11.190 --> 01:15:13.700
So you don't want to
lose all your progress.

01:15:13.700 --> 01:15:16.290
So you want to periodically
save your model to disk.

01:15:16.290 --> 01:15:19.730
And just to be very clear,
the thing you want to save

01:15:19.730 --> 01:15:24.620
is both the model,
and the optimizer

01:15:24.620 --> 01:15:28.350
and probably which iteration
you're on, you should add that.

01:15:28.350 --> 01:15:32.660
And then you can
just load it up.

01:15:32.660 --> 01:15:35.780
One, maybe final
note and I'll end

01:15:35.780 --> 01:15:40.300
is, I alluded to mix
precision training.

01:15:43.120 --> 01:15:46.620
Choice of the data type
has different trade-offs.

01:15:46.620 --> 01:15:51.320
If you have higher precision
it's more accurate and stable,

01:15:51.320 --> 01:15:55.970
but it's more expensive and
low precision is vice versa.

01:15:55.970 --> 01:16:00.260
And as we mentioned
before, by default,

01:16:00.260 --> 01:16:03.080
the recommendation
is use float32,

01:16:03.080 --> 01:16:08.160
but try to use bf16, or
even FP8 whenever possible.

01:16:08.160 --> 01:16:12.680
So you can use lower precision
for the feed forward pass,

01:16:12.680 --> 01:16:15.710
but float32 for the rest.

01:16:15.710 --> 01:16:19.050
And this is an idea that
goes back to the 2017.

01:16:19.050 --> 01:16:21.710
There's exploring mixed
precision training.

01:16:21.710 --> 01:16:24.680
Pytorch has some tools
that automatically

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:28.660
allow you to do mixed
precision training,

01:16:28.660 --> 01:16:31.510
because it can be
annoying to have

01:16:31.510 --> 01:16:34.090
to specify which
parts of your model

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:37.330
it needs to be what precision.

01:16:37.330 --> 01:16:41.390
Generally, you define your model
as this clean, modular thing

01:16:41.390 --> 01:16:46.090
and specifying the
precision is something

01:16:46.090 --> 01:16:47.925
that needs to cut across that.

01:16:50.530 --> 01:16:54.670
And I guess, maybe
one general comment

01:16:54.670 --> 01:16:58.240
is that people are
pushing the envelope

01:16:58.240 --> 01:17:00.200
on what precision is needed.

01:17:00.200 --> 01:17:02.950
There's some papers that
show you can actually

01:17:02.950 --> 01:17:06.600
use FP8 all the way through.

01:17:09.478 --> 01:17:12.465
I guess one of the
challenges is, of course,

01:17:12.465 --> 01:17:13.840
when you have
lower precision, it

01:17:13.840 --> 01:17:16.550
gets very numerically unstable.

01:17:16.550 --> 01:17:18.460
But then you can
do various tricks

01:17:18.460 --> 01:17:22.510
to control the
numerics of your model

01:17:22.510 --> 01:17:24.010
during training
so that you don't

01:17:24.010 --> 01:17:26.390
get into these bad regimes.

01:17:26.390 --> 01:17:30.910
So this is where I think
the systems and the model

01:17:30.910 --> 01:17:34.330
architecture design
are synergistic,

01:17:34.330 --> 01:17:39.610
because you want to design
models now that we have--

01:17:39.610 --> 01:17:42.680
a lot of model design is
just governed by hardware.

01:17:42.680 --> 01:17:45.200
So even the transformer,
as we mentioned last time,

01:17:45.200 --> 01:17:47.270
is governed by having GPUs.

01:17:47.270 --> 01:17:51.190
And now, if we notice
that NVIDIA chips have

01:17:51.190 --> 01:17:56.140
the property that if lower
precision, even like int4

01:17:56.140 --> 01:17:57.860
for example, is one thing.

01:17:57.860 --> 01:18:01.840
Now, if you can make your
model training actually

01:18:01.840 --> 01:18:05.930
work on int4, which
is I think quite hard,

01:18:05.930 --> 01:18:08.410
then you can get
massive speedups

01:18:08.410 --> 01:18:11.980
and your model will
be more efficient.

01:18:11.980 --> 01:18:15.820
Now, there's
another thing, which

01:18:15.820 --> 01:18:18.220
we'll talk about
later, which is often

01:18:18.220 --> 01:18:22.640
you'll train your model using
more sane floating point.

01:18:22.640 --> 01:18:25.380
But when it comes to
inference, you can go crazy

01:18:25.380 --> 01:18:27.660
and you take your
model, and then

01:18:27.660 --> 01:18:30.690
you can quantize it and
get a lot of the gains

01:18:30.690 --> 01:18:34.090
from very, very
aggressive quantization.

01:18:34.090 --> 01:18:37.080
So somehow training
is a lot more

01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:38.643
difficult to do
with low precision.

01:18:38.643 --> 01:18:40.060
But once you have
a trained model,

01:18:40.060 --> 01:18:44.160
it's much easier to
make it low precision.

01:18:44.160 --> 01:18:47.320
OK, so I will wrap up there.

01:18:47.320 --> 01:18:52.920
Just to conclude, we have talked
about the different primitives

01:18:52.920 --> 01:18:55.620
to use to train a model
building up from tensors all

01:18:55.620 --> 01:18:56.920
the way to the training loop.

01:18:56.920 --> 01:19:01.170
We talked about memory
accounting and flops accounting

01:19:01.170 --> 01:19:03.270
for these simple models.

01:19:03.270 --> 01:19:06.190
Hopefully, once you go
through assignment 1,

01:19:06.190 --> 01:19:08.370
all of these concepts
will be really solid

01:19:08.370 --> 01:19:11.610
because you'll be
applying these ideas

01:19:11.610 --> 01:19:14.010
for the actual transformer.

01:19:14.010 --> 01:19:15.150
OK.

01:19:15.150 --> 01:19:17.150
See you next time.
